Track 3: Proof Frame Closes Before the Bass Arrives

Story & Context: The EP closer begins from the physical clap of the wooden proofing frame. Malik records the sound but decides it must remain a live cue rather than a recurring sample. The rule is simple: the electronic sub may enter only after the frame is closed in real time.

Ilze opens with dry kokle harmonics and one low drone. Mara uses only paper-muted toy-piano taps, and Edgars contributes breath and key noise with no fully pitched notes. Malik builds the first two minutes from swung hand percussion, low toms, and claps with no sub-bass at all. When the proofing frame finally closes, the electronic low end enters beneath a bass-clarinet fundamental that is already sounding, making the machine appear to inherit weight from the acoustic reed.

Near the end, the frame is opened again and the sub disappears immediately. The quartet continues for one final cycle with kokle, bass-clarinet breath, and paper-stack percussion before the frame closes a second time in silence.

